---
import DocLayout from "../../components/docs/DocLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";
import ImagePlaceholder from "../../components/docs/ImagePlaceholder.astro";
---

<DocLayout
  title="Profiles"
  description="Learn how to use npkill profiles to clean up more than just node_modules - Python, Rust, Godot, and more."
>
  <h1>Profiles</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Profiles are one of the biggest features in npkill 1.0. They let you clean
    up much more than just <code>node_modules</code> — think Python virtual environments,
    Rust build artifacts, game engine caches, and more.
  </p>

  <h2>What are Profiles?</h2>
  <p>
    A profile is a collection of target directories that belong together.
    Instead of specifying individual targets, you can use a profile to search
    for all related directories at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, the <code>node</code> profile includes not just <code
      >node_modules</code
    >, but also <code>.next</code>, <code>.nx</code>, <code>.angular</code>, <code
      >.turbo</code
    >, and other heavy directories commonly found in Node.js projects.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some folders are found by name alone. Others only count when the files that
    identify their project are around them. For example, <code>target</code> is only
    shown next to a <code>Cargo.toml</code>, <code>pom.xml</code> or <code
      >build.sbt</code
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Using Profiles</h2>
  <p>Use the <code>-p</code> or <code>--profiles</code> flag:</p>

  <CodeBlock code="npx npkill -p python" />

  <p>Separate several profiles with commas:</p>

  <CodeBlock code="npx npkill -p node,python" />

  <p>
    By default, npkill uses the <code>node</code> profile. <code>all</code> combines
    every built-in profile, including your overrides of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Available Profiles</h2>
  <p>
    npkill 1.0 ships with 20 built-in profiles. Run <code
      >npkill --profiles</code
    > to see all of them.
  </p>

