  <ImagePlaceholder
    src="/img/profiles-result-counter.png"
    alt="npkill header with the result counter for the node profile"
    description="The result counter shows which profiles are active and how many items were found for each target type."
  />

  <h2>Profiles and Custom Targets</h2>
  <p>
    <code>--targets</code> searches only for the names you give and turns profiles
    off. It can't be combined with <code>-p</code>. To search for extra folders
    together with a profile, create your own profile in <a href="/docs/npkillrc"
      >.npkillrc</a
    >.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code='npx npkill --targets ".tmp,coverage"' />

  <h2>Custom Profiles</h2>
  <p>
    You can create your own profiles in the <a href="/docs/npkillrc"
      >.npkillrc</a
    > configuration file:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`{
  "profiles": {
    "webdev": {
      "description": "Frontend web development artifacts",
      "targets": ["dist", ".next", ".nuxt", ".output"]
    },
    "mystack": {
      "description": "Full-stack project artifacts (JS/Python/Java)",
      "targets": ["venv", ".venv", "target", "__pycache__", ".gradle"]
    },
    "mobile": {
      "description": "Mobile platform build folders and caches",
      "targets": ["Pods", "build", "DerivedData", "gradle"]
    }
  }
}`}
    lang="json"
    filename="~/.npkillrc"
  />

  <p>
    Custom profiles will override built-in profiles with the same name. <code
      >all</code
    > is reserved.
  </p>

  <h3 id="custom-rules">Custom rules</h3>
  <p>Targets take folder names and rules in the same list:</p>

  <CodeBlock
    code={`"targets": [
  "_opam",
  { "target": "_build", "nextTo": ["dune-project"] },
  { "target": "_build", "nextTo": ["dune-workspace"] }
]`}
    lang="json"
  />

  <ul>
    <li>
      A name finds every folder called that way. <code>--targets</code> works the
      same.
    </li>
    <li>
      A rule needs <code>nextTo</code>, <code>contains</code> or both. Every entry
      listed must exist.
    </li>
    <li>Rules with the same target are alternatives. Any of them is enough.</li>
    <li>
      Entries are plain names compared exactly, case included. Paths and the
      characters <code>* ? [ ] {"{"} {"}"} / \</code> are rejected.
    </li>
    <li>Unknown properties are rejected.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why Profiles Matter</h2>
  <p>
    Before npkill 1.0, you had to manually specify targets or run npkill
    multiple times for different project types. Profiles solve this by grouping
    related directories together, making it easy to clean up entire development
    stacks with a single command.
  </p>

  <div class="tip">
    <i class="bx bx-bulb"></i>
    <div>
      <strong>Pro tip:</strong> Set <code>defaultProfiles</code> in your <code
        >.npkillrc</code
      >
      so npkill always uses your preferred profiles.
    </div>
  </div>
</DocLayout>

